# ai-dev-baseline — line-ending policy (#2).
#
# Windows is supported via WSL2 only, and the one thing WSL genuinely adds to a shell-heavy repo
# is a checkout hazard: a clone made by WINDOWS git with core.autocrlf=true, then executed from
# WSL, gives every script `\r` line endings. The failure is `bash: $'\r': command not found` on
# all 59 entry points at once, with nothing naming the cause.
#
# install.sh preflights for it (adb_crlf_scan), but a preflight is a net, not a guarantee — a
# fully corrupted checkout cannot even run the preflight, because ./install.sh dies on its own
# `bash\r` shebang first. THIS file is the guarantee: it takes the hazard away for a fresh clone
# regardless of the user's core.autocrlf, so the net only ever has to catch a clone that predates
# it or was repaired by hand.
#
# `eol=lf` implies `text`, but both are written out: the pairing is what makes the intent legible
# to the next reader, and `text` alone would normalize on commit while leaving checkout to the
# platform.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# The extensionless commands. `bin/agent-init` and `bin/baseline` are shell scripts with no `.sh`,
# so a `*.sh`-only policy silently exempts the two files a user is most likely to run first.
bin/agent-init  text eol=lf
bin/baseline    text eol=lf

# Shell files carried inside agent payloads and this repo's own hooks are matched by the `*.sh`
# rule above; listed here as a reminder that the rule is deliberately tree-wide rather than scoped
# to scripts/.
